TY  - JOUR
TI  - Characterization of Three Surges of the Kyagar Glacier, Karakoram
AU  - Zhen Zhang
PY  - 2023
JO  - Remote Sensing
DO  - 10.3390/rs15082113
AB  - Glaciers experience periodic variations in flow velocity called surges, each of which influences the glacier’s characteristics and the occurrence of downstream disasters. The Karakoram region contains many surging glaciers, yet there are few comprehensive studies of multiple surge cycles. In this study, Landsat, topographic map, SRTM, TerraSAR-X/TanDEM-X, ITS_LIVE and Sentinel-1 glacier velocity data were used to analyse the characteristics of Kyagar Glacier since the 1970s. Three surging events were identified, with active phases of 1975–1978, 1995–1997 and 2014–2016. The timing of these surges was similar, with a cycle of 19–20 years, active phase of 3–4 years, buildup phase of 4–5 years, and depletion phase of 11–13 years. During the depletion phase, a large amount of ice accumulates in the reservoir zone at the top of the glacier tongue, and the terminal of the tongue thins significantly. During the buildup phase, the reservoir zone thins slightly, the tongue begins to thicken, and the flow rate begins to increase. Glacier flow accelerates suddenly in the active phase, with a maximum velocity of 2 m d-1 observed. Then, the glacier terminal thickens sharply, the reservoir zone thins by 12 m, and the terminal receiving zone thickens by 28 m. In the most recent surge, the glacier may have entered a depletion phase after July 2016 when a glacial lake outburst (GLOF) event occurred. Each surge causes 7–8 GLOF events, which occur in the active phase and early depletion phase. Active-phase GLOFs occur when ineffective drainage channels begin to become effective. Depletion-phase GLOFs are caused by the melting of ice transferred from the reservoir zone to the receiving zone during a surge.
