TY  - JOUR
TI  - A GF-3 imagery-based dataset of glacier surface motion distribution in High Mountain Asia during 2019–2020
AU  - Qimin Zhang
PY  - 2021
JO  - China Scientific Data
DO  - 10.11922/csdata.2021.0028.zh
AB  - The Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, also known as the world’s “Third Pole”, is the largest modern glaciation region in the middle and low latitudes of the Northern Hemisphere, which plays an important role in the global climate system. It is of great significance to study the spatiotemporal changes of the glacier motion for understanding the dynamic characteristics of glaciers and its response to climate change. Besides, the long-term monitoring of glacier activity on the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau plays an important guiding role in promoting regional sustainable development and global ecological environment protection. This dataset is based on the SAR imagery of the High Mountain Asia acquired by GF-3 from 2019 to 2020. We adopted an improved pixel-tracking algorithm for calculation to obtain the dataset (GeoTiff format in 32-bit floating-point) of the distribution of glacier surface motion by precisely removing the global deformation, the topographic-related offset and the effect of the high-frequency noise. We sampled the SAR image of GF-3 satellite FSI imaging mode to keep the spatial resolution of the results around 12 m. The statistical analysis of the residual movement in the non-glacial region indicates that the overall accuracy is about 0.5 m. The results indicate that the glacier motion in the accumulation area is generally faster than that in the terminal or ablation part, and some small and medium-sized glaciers have relatively strong activities. Generally, the glacier surface motion reveals a positive correlation with topography and climate. The dataset can serve as the basis in the research on glacial mass balance in Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, provide statistical support to understand the relationship between ice and climate change in High Mountain Asia, and promote the in-depth application of domestic remote sensing data in glacier activity and climate change.
