TY  - JOUR
TI  - Linking rain-on-snow events to river floods in the Tianshan Mountains
AU  - Fan Sun
PY  - 2026
JO  - Weather and Climate Extremes
DO  - 10.1016/j.wace.2026.100968
AB  - Rain-on-snow (ROS) events are widely recognized as an important cryosphere–hydrology coupling mechanism, yet when and where ROS events translate into river floods remains poorly constrained, particularly in arid high-mountain regions. Here, we provide the first basin-scale assessment of potential flood-inducing ROS (ROS-PF) and actual river-flood-generating ROS (ROS-AF) events across the Tianshan Mountains. Using ERA5-Land reanalysis (1951–2019) and an observation-based reconstructed daily streamflow dataset covering 40 Tianshan watersheds, we link ROS forcing to reconstructed high-flow responses. ROS-PF events—defined by rainfall ≥10 mm occurring on snowpack with snow water equivalent ≥10 mm—are concentrated in spring (April–June) and mid-elevations (2.0–3.5 km), within a narrow temperature window of–0.24 ± 1.49 °C. Their frequency has increased significantly over the past seven decades, driven primarily by rising heavy rainfall occurrence rather than snow availability. On average, ROS-PF events contributed on average 14.7% of the 6-day snowmelt total and 8.3% of the 6-day available-water total across the Tianshan basins. Crucially, only ∼44% of ROS-PF events evolve into ROS-AF events, revealing strong basin-dependent conversion efficiency. Some basins like Pochengzi basin convert nearly all ROS-PF events into floods, while others exhibit high ROS-PF frequency but minimal flood response. Most ROS-AF events occur with a lag of up to six days, underscoring the importance of delayed hydrological responses. These findings demonstrate that ROS does not uniformly amplify flood risk but instead reorganizes flood-generation pathways under warming, with implications for flood forecasting and water management in arid mountain systems.
ER  - 

