TY  - JOUR
TI  - Environmental Sustainability and Adaptations in Indus Basin
AU  - Shahana Maryam
PY  - 2024
JO  - Evolutionary Studies in Imaginative Culture
DO  - 10.70082/esiculture.vi.724
AB  - The Indus Basin's advantageous climate and topography rendered it a prime site for the implementation of an irrigation system. This study looks at how the Indus Basin is affected by climate change. Changes in water flow, glacial/snow melt, and catchment areas in the Indus and its tributaries are revealed by trend analysis from hydro-logical stations. A network of canals constituted of trans-boundary rivers was built beside the Indus River System (IRS), soon after the implementation of the Indus Water Treaty, about 70% of its water was allocated for irrigation purposes. An evident increase from 8.5 to 18.2 million hectares (MH) in the cropland area in Pakistan is monitored, whereas in India it increased widely from 2.02 to 8.5 MH that is about three times of the pre-vious figures due to expansion of the Indus irrigation network. Water tables have dropped and water logging has resulted from urbanization and groundwater extraction, while the use of pesticides and fertilizers has lowered the quality of the water.These problems are made worse by climate change, which presents serious obstacles to the region's water security, environmental sustainability, and economic growth.This article provides a comprehensive analysis of the modifications in the Indus Basin irrigation system and the impact of these changes on environmental resources. We can create practical plans to lessen the consequences of climate change and guarantee the Indus Basin has a safe and sustainable water supply in the future by comprehending how it affects the basin.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Hydrological Uncertainty Across Snow‐ and Rainfall‐Dominated Sub‐Basins of a Partially Glacierized Himalayan Catchment
AU  - Nikunj K. Mangukiya
PY  - 2026
JO  - Hydrological Processes
DO  - 10.1002/hyp.70717
AB  - ABSTRACT Uncertainty in hydrological modelling is often addressed through parameter estimation, while uncertainties from model inputs and structure remain underexplored. This limitation is critical in mountainous catchments, where sparse monitoring and high spatial heterogeneity amplify uncertainty. This study assesses precipitation input, parameter and model structure uncertainties in the Alaknanda River Basin, a partially glacierized Himalayan catchment with snow‐dominated upstream and rainfall‐driven downstream sub‐basins. Precipitation input and parameter uncertainties were evaluated using the SWAT+ model through Latin Hypercube Sampling and the Sequential Uncertainty Fitting algorithm, while model structure uncertainty was assessed using 47 conceptual hydrological models implemented in the MARRMoT framework. The precipitation input assessment shows that differences among precipitation datasets substantially affect simulated streamflow, particularly in areas with high precipitation variability. Parameter sensitivity analysis indicates that snow‐related parameters exert strong control on simulated streamflows in the upper basin, with parameter uncertainty producing the widest prediction uncertainty band at the glacier‐fed Joshimath station ( r ‐factor 0.85). The model structure assessment shows substantial variation among conceptual model structures, with wider prediction bands at the downstream rainfall‐ and baseflow‐dominated sub‐basins ( r ‐factor 0.63 at Rudraprayag‐A). Structural analysis further indicates that snow storage, soil moisture and routing stores are important for reliable simulations, whereas additional storage components do not consistently improve performance. Overall, the three uncertainty assessments reveal distinct spatial patterns associated with the dominant hydrological processes across the basin. These findings highlight the need for process‐specific calibration, data collection and model selection to improve hydrological predictions in complex mountainous catchments.
