TY  - JOUR
TI  - Snowmelt runoff contribution to streamflow in the Karakoram alpine basin system
AU  - Youssef Benali
PY  - 2025
JO  - International Journal of Geography Geology and Environment
DO  - 10.22271/27067483.2025.v7.i7c.727
AB  - The Karakoram Range feeds some of Asia's most important rivers the Indus, Shyok, and Hunza yet the relative contribution of snowmelt to their total annual flow remains poorly quantified, partly because high-altitude monitoring stations are few and far between. This research used a combination of field-measured snow water equivalent (SWE) data, temperature-index snowmelt modelling, and hydrograph separation to estimate the snowmelt contribution to streamflow across three alpine basins in the central Karakoram, Pakistan, over a two-year period (2021-2022). Snow courses were established at 12 stations spanning 3,200 to 5,400 m elevation, with monthly SWE measurements supplemented by daily temperature and precipitation records from automatic weather stations. The Snowmelt Runoff Model (SRM) was calibrated against observed discharge at three gauging points and validated against an independent year of data. Results indicated that snowmelt accounted for 43.7% of total annual streamflow on average across the three basins, with glacier melt contributing 24.8%, rainfall runoff 18.3%, and baseflow 13.2%. Peak snowmelt occurred between late April and mid-June, preceding the glacier melt peak by 4-6 weeks. SWE at the onset of the melt season was the strongest predictor of subsequent streamflow volume (r² = 0.76). The snowmelt fraction increased with mean basin elevation, from 36.4% at 3,800 m mean elevation to 52.1% at 4,900 m. These partitioning estimates are needed for water resource planning in downstream communities that depend on predictable melt-season flows for irrigation and hydropower.
