AU  - Huaijun Wang
PY  - 2026
JO  - Journal of Hydrology Regional Studies
DO  - 10.1016/j.ejrh.2026.103553
AB  - Study region: Tianshan Mountain, Central Asia. Study Focus: This study evaluates the performance of four precipitation and four evapotranspiration datasets in the data-scarce Tianshan Mountain during 1981–2023. Based on the selected datasets, an Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) model combined with SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) is applied to simulate runoff and identify the key factors controlling runoff and extreme runoff in two typical basins. New Hydrological Insights for the Region: MSWEP precipitation shows superior performance compared with PERSIANN, ERA5-Land, and CHIRPS, while hpet performs best among ERA5pet, CAMELE, and GLEAMpet. Despite similar warming and evapotranspiration trends in the Kaidu and Kumarak Rivers, their hydrological responses differ substantially: the Kaidu River exhibits increased soil moisture and decreased snow water equivalent, whereas the Kumarak River displays the opposite pattern. The XGBoost model achieves high accuracy in daily runoff simulations. Based on SHAP values, in the Kaidu River, temperature contributes most to daily runoff (49%), while precipitation is the dominant driver of extreme runoff (30%). In contrast, soil moisture is the strongest predictor of both runoff and extreme runoff in the Kumarak River, contributing 75% and 33% to runoff and extreme runoff, respectively. Our findings demonstrate that integrating reanalysis datasets with machine learning can improve understanding of runoff drivers in data-scarce regions and highlight the need for finer spatiotemporal hydroclimatological observations to reduce model uncertainties.
