TI  - Dust-modified precipitation influences glacier mass-balance patterns across High Mountain Asia
AU  - Mao Xing-li
PY  - 2026
JO  - Advances in Climate Change Research
DO  - 10.1016/j.accre.2026.10.003
AB  - The atmospheric pathway through which dust modifies glacier mass balance by altering precipitation remains poorly quantified, limiting a comprehensive understanding of dust–glacier interactions across High Mountain Asia (HMA). Here, we coupled the Weather Research and Forecasting with Chemistry (WRF-Chem) and glacier energy‒mass balance simulations to quantify the effects of dust on glacier mass-balance in HMA during 2016‒2022. Our results reveal that dust influences glacier mass balance primarily by altering solid precipitation (63%) rather than albedo reduction (34%). Dust increases snowfall in Syr Darya‒Amu Darya basins but suppresses it in Tarim–Indus basins, creating opposing mass-balance responses. Omitting dust effects underestimates mass loss by 13%–24%, with direct implications for water-resource projections. These findings extend the conventional view of dust as primarily a surface-darkening agent and highlight the importance of incorporating dust‒precipitation interactions into future assessments of glacier change and associated water resources across HMA.
