TI  - Glacier Mass Balance Trends in High-Mountain Asia Over the Past Three Decades
AU  - Lieve Bianchi
PY  - 2023
JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - 10.5281/zenodo.22801909
AB  - Glaciers in High-Mountain Asia represent one of the largest reservoirs of freshwater outside the polar regions, sustaining river systems that support hundreds of millions of people across South and Central Asia. This study presents a comprehensive analysis of glacier mass balance trends across High-Mountain Asia over the past three decades, synthesizing satellite geodetic measurements, in-situ glaciological observations, and regional climate reanalysis data. Our results reveal pronounced spatial heterogeneity in mass balance trends, with glaciers in the eastern and central Himalaya, as well as much of the Tien Shan and Pamir periphery, exhibiting accelerating mass loss, while portions of the Karakoram and western Kunlun Shan display near-balanced or slightly positive mass budgets—a pattern consistent with the so-called Karakoram Anomaly. We attribute these divergent trends to regional differences in precipitation seasonality, debris cover extent, and the relative influence of westerly versus monsoonal moisture sources. Temporal analysis indicates that the rate of regional mass loss has accelerated notably since the mid-2000s, coincident with rising air temperatures and shifts in precipitation phase. We discuss the implications of these findings for downstream water availability, glacial lake outburst flood hazards, and long-term water security in glacier-fed river basins, emphasizing the need for continued high-resolution monitoring to constrain future projections of glacier change in this critically important region.
