AU  - Suresh Aluvihara
PY  - 2025
JO  - International Journal of Atmospheric and Oceanic Sciences
DO  - 10.11648/j.ijaos.20250902.15
AB  - The Hindu Kush Himalaya (HKH) region faces a growing crisis of air and atmospheric pollution, driven by a complex interplay of factors including rapid urbanization, industrial emissions, agricultural practices, and transboundary pollution. This pollution, encompassing particulate matter, ozone, and other harmful gases, is having devastating consequences across the region. On human health, the polluted air contributes significantly to respiratory illnesses, cardiovascular diseases, and even premature deaths. Vulnerable populations, such as children and the elderly, are particularly susceptible. The region's ecosystems are also under severe stress. Air pollution disrupts plant growth and biodiversity, affecting the delicate ecological balance of the mountains. Deposition of pollutants alters soil chemistry and water quality, impacting forest health and the survival of many species. Water resources, crucial for the livelihoods of millions, are threatened by atmospheric deposition of pollutants. Acid rain and the settling of particulate matter contaminate rivers, lakes, and glaciers, affecting water availability and quality for drinking and irrigation. Agricultural activities are also adversely affected. Air pollution damages crops, reduces yields, and contaminates food sources, impacting food security and farmer livelihoods. Soot deposition accelerates glacier melt, further exacerbating water scarcity and increasing the risk of glacial lake outburst floods. The HKH region's vulnerability to climate change is amplified by air pollution, demanding urgent and coordinated action to mitigate these adverse impacts. Finally, the review emphasizes the need for integrated mitigation strategies and further research to protect the health and livelihoods of the millions of people dependent on the HKH region.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Beyond greenness: Combining MODIS vegetation trajectories and GEDI canopy structure to reveal contrasting structural states across the Hindu Kush Himalaya
AU  - Niti B. Mishra
PY  - 2026
JO  - International Journal of Applied Earth Observation and Geoinformation
DO  - 10.1016/j.jag.2026.105586
AB  - Satellite greening is often interpreted as vegetation recovery, productivity gain, or woody expansion, yet optical vegetation indices alone cannot show whether greening reflects low-stature vegetation, developing woody cover, mature forest, biomass accumulation or structurally simplified canopy. This ambiguity is especially important in mountain regions, where climate, snow, topography and disturbance vary sharply over short distances. This study combined MODIS Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) and Enhanced Vegetation Index (EVI) trajectories from 2001 to 2024 with contemporary 1-km estimates of canopy height, aboveground biomass density, and vertical structural complexity from the Global Ecosystem Dynamics Investigation (GEDI) to develop a spaceborne remote sensing framework to characterize the contemporary structural context of satellite-observed woody vegetation greening across the Hindu Kush Himalaya. Valid woody pixels were classified into six spectral–structural states representing low-stature greening, woody structural greening, complex or high-biomass greening, mature woody browning, stable vegetation with hidden structural complexity, and simplified green forest. The environmental and disturbance settings of these classes were then evaluated using snow, temperature, precipitation, moisture stress, topography, forest loss, fire frequency, and nighttime lights. Greening occurred across a wide structural range, from low-stature vegetation to tall, high-biomass, structurally complex forest. Complex or high-biomass greening was associated with relatively moist and precipitation-favorable environments, whereas mature woody browning occurred in structurally developed landscapes experiencing increasing thermal and moisture stress and elevated forest loss. Spectrally stable but structurally complex vegetation showed comparatively high disturbance signals, although this class was rare. Simplified green forest was concentrated in colder, snow-influenced, relatively dry, and topographically constrained areas with little recent forest loss or fire, suggesting environmental limitation rather than recent degradation. Spatial process-context models distinguished the major classes with cross-validated ROC AUC values of 0.739–0.893. These results show that similar greenness trajectories can represent markedly different canopy conditions. Contemporary lidar structure therefore provides essential context for interpreting long-term optical trends, although it does not directly measure structural change over the full 2001–2024 period. The framework offers a more ecologically informative approach for evaluating mountain vegetation change than greenness trends alone.
